      "lab_synopsis": "The Schneider Lab investigates how selective regulation of mRNA stability and translation controls cancer metastasis, immune cell differentiation, and tissue regeneration, with an emphasis on developing RNA-targeted therapeutics. The group defines how non-canonical and cap-dependent translation pathways remodel cellular proteomes in response to stress, inflammation, and oncogenic signaling. Their studies identified DAP5/eIF4G2-dependent translation as critical for Treg stability and immune tolerance, while eIF4E-driven translation promotes T follicular helper (TFH) differentiation and autoimmune pathology. In cancer, the lab uncovered translational programs that enable metastasis and survival under hypoxia and metabolic stress. Current efforts focus on mapping translation factor networks and RNA regulons that control immune and tumor cell states, and leveraging these insights to design selective translational inhibitors and RNA-based therapeutics.",
