      "specialty_areas": "Health Equity Research",
      "lab_synopsis": "I am a social work–trained implementation scientist with over ten years of experience conducting research on integrated care in global settings. My work focuses on evidence-informed implementation science to address the dual burden of communicable and noncommunicable diseases in low-resource, high-need contexts, with an emphasis on community-engaged and partnership-driven approaches. My research centers on integrating services for comorbid conditions, building healthcare workforce capacity, engaging affected populations in intervention adaptation, and delivering contextually tailored implementation strategies. I have contributed to domestic and international research addressing HIV/AIDS, tuberculosis, substance use, and cardiovascular disease. My work has been published in leading journals, including Implementation Science, Progress in Cardiovascular Diseases, and European Heart Journal. As an Assistant Professor in Global Health, I apply implementation science frameworks to scale evidence-based interventions for hypertension and HIV comorbidity, incorporating stakeholder engagement strategies such as human-centered design and differentiated service delivery models. I serve as Co-Investigator on the MAP-IT study in Akwa-Ibom, Nigeria, focused on integrated hypertension and HIV care, and am Principal Investigator of a K01 career development award (K01HL165086) to co-develop and evaluate a community–clinical linkage strategy to improve care for individuals living with HIV and hypertension in resource-constrained settings.",
