      "specialty_areas": "Health Equity Research",
      "lab_synopsis": "I am a Research Professor with strong strengthening health systems and policy training background cross-cutting areas around mental health and substance use treatment, prevention, policy development with a strong focus on vulnerable contexts and populations. I have over 15 years of experience in cross-cultural psychopathology and mental health and substance use interventions research in low resource contexts. More recently I am working with WHO’s Department of Mental Health/Brain Health and Substance Use on two areas: developing monitoring and evaluation frameworks to advance population mental health and substance use surveillance and developing community mental health programs in diverse geographies to address ‘treatment gap’.   I will bring to this study my 10 years of experience in carrying out mental health services strengthening and developing policy relevant messages from evidence based mental health services research in low resource contexts. Some data monitoring reports where I have been a scientific contributor include  a)\tGuidance on mental health policy and strategic action plans. Geneva: World Health Organization (WHO); 2025 (Guidance on mental health policy and strategic action plans). b) Global report on children with developmental disabilities: from the margins to the mainstream. Geneva: World Health Organization and the United Nations Children’s Fund (UNICEF), 2023, c) WHO. Guide for integration of perinatal mental health in maternal and child health services. Geneva: World Health Organization; 2022, d) United Nations Children’s Fund, The State of the World’s Children 2021: On My Mind – Promoting, protecting and caring for children’s mental health, UNICEF, New York, October 2021. The proposed study ‘Integrating IEHE and DPH Data Hubs to advance understanding of comorbidities of Mental and Substance Use Disorders: Accelerating an embedded learning health ecosystem’ will help advance understanding of how mental health and substance use related surveillance and clinical management can be advanced within NYU health care systems and will provide IEHE with additional tools and evidence to integrate disparate datahubs to harmonize and learn from mental health and substance use related data sources that may exist within different hubs. It is also a pathfinder demonstration project that may help in improving understanding of the use, quality, workflows of existing screening tools and assessment processes leading to management of comorbid conditions like Substance use and mental disorders. It is a project also undertaken by a researcher with largely an LMIC and health equity focus to explore how disparities and health complexities can be enhanced within NYU care landscape using lessons from low resource settings.",
