      "specialty_areas": "Health Equity Research",
      "lab_synopsis": "Dr. Divers is Professor of Biostatistics and Director of the Division of Health Services Research in the Department of Foundations of Medicine at the NYU Grossman Long Island School of Medicine. He is a biostatistician with expertise in statistical genetics. Dr. Divers has a broad research portfolio that includes chronic kidney disease and end-stage kidney disease projects, kidney transplant outcomes, type 1 diabetes, type 2 diabetes mellitus, cognitive function, cerebrovascular disease, and cardiovascular disease. This is a proposed randomized controlled trial (RCT) to evaluate strategies for safely tapering patients of Glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1) after achieving and maintaining the weight loss goals. Obesity affects over 40% of U.S. adults and significantly increases the risk of type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular disease. GLP-1’s have been shown to be effective at producing significant weight loss among overweight or obese patients. However, over 50% of patients initiated on a GLP-1 discontinuity the therapy within one year. It is unknown whether nutrition counseling and medically tailored meals can help maintain weight loss for overweight or obese patients during GLP-1 tapering or discontinuation. Before a definitive trial can be launched, it is essential to determine whether combining nutrition counseling and MTMs with a GLP-1 taper is feasible, acceptable, and operationally implementable in a real-world clinical setting. The SUSTAIN-RD (SUSTAINing GLP-1 Benefits with Medically Tailored Meals and registered dietitian counseling support is designed to address these questions. This randomized controlled pilot trial will evaluate the feasibility and acceptability of a multilayered nutrition intervention during and after GLP-1 tapering, to inform a future large-scale trial. The trial’s specific aims are to: (1) determine the feasibility of the SUSTAIN-RD pilot trial by assessing the following metrics: 1) eligibility, consent, enrollment rate, and retention rates; and (2) evaluate the acceptability of the SUSTAIN-RD trial, using quantitative and qualitative measures; and (3) generate preliminary estimates of weight and cardiometabolic changes to guide effect-size and power calculations for a future RCT. This pilot will provide data to inform a future full-scale trial to rigorously evaluate the efficacy and implementation of the intervention, including recruitment and enrollment strategies, trial design (e.g., sample size), and outcomes. I have served as PI or MPI on multiple NIH grants and Cooperative Agreements, including CASSIDY (1U18DK145716) and DiCAYA (U18DP006510). I am currently serving as the Data Coordinating Center lead for the TwoPlus trial (CER-2022C1-26300), a PCORI-funded multicenter trial to evaluate the comparative effectiveness of an individualized model of hemodialysis vs conventional hemodialysis. Dr. Divers will lead analytical efforts for the proposed trial and work in collaboration with Dr. Zhang and Dr. Lau. We have assembled an outstanding multidisciplinary team to work on this project. I have considerable expertise in all areas needed to address these questions, including study design, biostatistics, clinical trial management, and data analysis. Dr. Divers has the skills, experience, leadership, and motivation necessary to lead this proposal successfully",
