      "specialty_areas": "Health Equity Research",
      "lab_synopsis": "The overall objective of this study is to identify early digital markers of glycemic and metabolic dysregulation in 200 trauma survivors using wearable technology and machine learning (ML) algorithms. As a Tenured Professor in the Departments of Emergency Medicine and Surgery at the NYU Grossman School of Medicine and Bellevue Hospital Center, I have the expertise, leadership, training, and motivation necessary to serve as a Co-Investigator for the proposed research. I have a broad background in health services outcomes and educational research methods. I have specific training and expertise in mixed methods, ethnographic, qualitative, and survey research and data analysis techniques including econometric modeling, program evaluation, and quantitative analysis of qualitative data. My research interests are in improving health behaviors of underserved populations using digital educational media and using modern surveillance techniques to identify problem areas amenable to intervention. As a Co-investigator on our collaborative New York State Funded traffic injury surveillance grants, I served as the methodologist and emergency medicine liaison supervising the data analysis and monitoring collaborations among emergency department providers to ensure recruitment targets were met. I also served as a site Principal Investigator for Identifying Road Hazards for Bicyclists, a study funded by the Insurance Institute for Highway Safety. Our work has led to significant contributions to the pedestrian and bicyclist injury literature with this project building upon our prior success. I also am contact-PI for a mixed methods project that is attempting to recover lungs from patients who die unexpectedly in emergency departments (R33HL156890, PIs Wall, Montgomery). Specific to this project, I have an established collaborative relationship with Dr. Schultebraucks who is serving as PI for this proposal. Currently, I serve as a Co-Investigator on multiple initiatives focusing on the importance of well-being and mental health in healthcare workers and patients experiencing various levels of posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD). Dr. Schultebraucks and I collaborate on NIH-funded R01HL156134, which analyzes expressive flexibility in healthcare workers and the linkage of the improvement of emotion regulation and resilience. Additionally, we collaborate on R01MH129856, which looks at large language models that predicts PTSD risk in patients, as many trauma survivors are impacted by PTSD post discharge. This work highlights the importance of using the ED as a vital point for early risk stratification, and carries well into the proposed research, building off of the infrastructure of our NIH-funded study of trauma survivors admitted to the ED after a potential traumatic event (R01MH129856). The addition of wearables and standard glycemic assessments will only further support the ED as a point of early detection of trauma, and mitigation of adverse metabolic outcomes in the long term.",
