      "specialty_areas": "Health Equity Research",
      "lab_synopsis": "My mission as a scientist is to conduct interdisciplinary lifestyle interventions targeting obesity. In particular, my future research niche bridges the gap between biological and behavioral determinants associated with weight loss maintenance. As my long-term career goal, I aim to test novel strategies (e.g., time-restricted eating) to attenuate weight regain following successful weight loss. My doctoral training and research experience as a postdoctoral fellow have provided me with a broad background in exercise physiology and nutritional science, with specific training in the assessment of body composition (bioelectrical impedance analysis and dualenergy x-ray absorptiometry), exercise testing (Wingate anaerobic tests, graded exercise treadmill), energy expenditure (resting energy expenditure) and physical activity using free-living wearable devices (Sensewear, Actigraph). As a postdoctoral fellow, I expanded my research and interdisciplinary efforts to focus on adults with obesity and diabetes. Specifically, I served as a fellow on the American Heart Association-funded Personal Diet study, a bio-behavioral weight loss intervention in individuals with prediabetes and early-stage type 2 diabetes. I served as a co-investigator (PI: Sevick [retired]) and, currently, as the principal investigator on an NINR-funded award examining the impact of a precision nutrition intervention for the treatment of type 2 diabetes (R01NR018916). I was awarded a K99/R00 Pathway to Independence Career Development Award (K99HL163474) to examine the efficacy of timerestricted eating, a lifestyle intervention, for weight-loss maintenance in adults with obesity who have recently lost weight. Relevant to this application, I have extensive expertise in lifestyle interventions targeting weight loss, with experience measuring self-monitoring behaviors. I am well-suited to serve as a co-Investigator on this application entitled, 'Intensive behavioral support to improve adherence and outcomes for patients taking incretin mimetics for obesity.\"",
