      "specialty_areas": "African American health / CARDIA cohort / HPA axis / Life's Essential 8 / Life's Simple 7",
      "lab_synopsis": "Dr. Ortiz is a physician-scientist that has been recognized as an international expert on the interface between relational health and intergenerational cardiovascular health. For example, she was awarded a prestigious Harold Amos Medical Faculty Program Award to study, “Exploring the Association Between the Caregiver-Child Relationship and Adult Cardiovascular Health: Implications for [reframing Adverse Childhood Experiences to] Positive Dyadic Experiences”. The premise of this work is that the defining characteristics of being a caregiver (positive dyadic experiences) can be harnessed to sustainably drive change in CVH at the level of the adult and lay the foundation for the CVH of their children. In her early career, she has already authored over 40 peer-reviewed manuscripts and was recently named a 40-Under-40 Leader in Health by the National Minority Quality Forum. Due to her unique experience and critical expertise, she has been selected to inform national policy related to relational health. She was one of the first scientific advisors to join the ACEs (Adverse Childhood Experiences) Aware Initiative through the Aurrera Health Group in partnership with The Office of the Surgeon General of California, Nadine Burke Harris. She has continued this work as she currently serves as a scientific advisor with particular emphasis on the area of health equity to the UCLA-UCSF ACEs Aware Family Resilience Network (UCAAN). One of her key roles is in sitting on a task force of experts convened to inform an algorithm in response to the identification and treatment of toxic stress. She most recently was invited to sit on the New York State Trauma Informed Resource Network (TIRN) Advisory Council, serving an important role at a time when New York state begins work to build relational health programming and policy including ACEs screening response.   Dr. Ortiz is an Assistant Professor of Pediatrics and Population Health and a faculty member in the Institute for Excellence in Health Equity at New York University Grossman School of Medicine. Dr. Ortiz is triple-board certified in internal medicine, pediatrics, and lifestyle medicine. She is the founder of the Child-Caregiver Heart Health Program. Her research program aims to 1) understand the mechanisms underpinning the association between stress physiology and cardiometabolic disease risk, and 2) identify and promote approaches to the attainment of optimized intergenerational cardiovascular health for disease prevention, with a focus on adults who are caregivers of children.   Throughout all of this work, she maintains clinical practice working with marginalized communities at Bellevue Hospital in the largest public hospital system in the country, New York City Health+Hospitals. She also serves as a true advocate and mentor of trainees across disciplines and educational programs.     Dr. Ortiz obtained her Bachelor of Science in behavioral neuroscience from Northeastern University, her medical degree from Loyola University Stritch School of Medicine in Chicago, and her residency training at the Johns Hopkins Hospital. Dr. Ortiz has also received research training at the National Institutes for Health in Bethesda, Maryland. Before joining NYU as faculty, she completed the prestigious National Clinician Scholars Program at the University of Pennsylvania and The Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia, where she simultaneously obtained a Master of Science degree in health policy research.",
